            <ArticleTitle>Gastrointestinal Fistulization in Amebic Liver Abscess.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>253-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Liver abscess is a common deep seated abscess in children; amebic liver abscess is associated with more local complications.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE CHARACTERISTICS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We report two preschool children presenting with short history of pain, fever and right upper quadrant pain. The abscess communicated with gastro-intestinal tract (ascending colon in case 1 and duodenum in case 2), and diagnosis of amebic liver abscess was confirmed by DNA PCR.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OUTCOME" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Both children were successfully managed with intravenous antibiotics and catheter drainage.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MESSAGE" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Gastrointestinal fistulization may be rarely seen in amebic liver abscess. Conservative management with antibiotics, catheter drainage and supportive care may suffice.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
